    <header>
        <h1>Computer Networks: The Foundation of Digital Communication</h1>
    </header>

    <main>

        <section class="hero">
            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1516321318423-f06f85e504b3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80" alt="Computer networking and digital communication">
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Introduction</h2>
            <p>
                Computer networks form the foundation of modern digital communication by enabling devices
                to exchange information efficiently and securely. Organizations, educational institutions,
                and individuals rely on networks to share resources, access the internet, and support
                communication services.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Network Devices</h2>
            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80" alt="Network devices and server equipment">
            <p>
                End devices such as computers, smartphones, printers, and servers generate and receive data.
                Switches connect devices within a LAN, routers connect multiple networks, and firewalls
                protect systems from unauthorized access and cyber threats.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Transmission Media</h2>
            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1526379095098-d400fd0bf935?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80" alt="Digital technology and data transmission">
            <p>
                Transmission media carry data between devices. Wired media include Ethernet and
                fiber-optic cables, while wireless media use radio waves. Fiber optics provide
                high bandwidth and excellent reliability.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Types of Networks</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>LAN:</strong> Connects devices within a local area.</li>
                <li><strong>WAN:</strong> Connects networks across large geographic regions.</li>
                <li><strong>WLAN:</strong> Uses wireless technology such as Wi-Fi.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Networking Concepts</h2>
            <p>
                IP addressing provides unique identifiers to network devices. Switching directs
                data within local networks, while routing determines the best path between
                different networks. TCP/IP enables reliable communication across the internet.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Conclusion</h2>
            <p>
                Computer networks rely on devices, transmission media, and communication protocols
                to exchange information effectively. Understanding networking fundamentals is
                essential for modern technology users and IT professionals.
            </p>
        </section>

    </main>

